<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<h2>Why commercial appraisal is different</h2>
<p>Commercial forms are less standardized than homeowner policies. They may include coinsurance, separate deductibles for wind or earthquake, ordinance-or-law coverage, and business-income provisions with their own waiting periods. The appraisal clause sits inside all of that, so we read the whole policy before valuing a single square foot.</p>
<h2>What we value</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Low-slope roofs:</strong> membrane, built-up and metal systems on warehouses and retail centers, including insulation, edge metal and rooftop equipment curbs.</li>
<li><strong>Building envelope:</strong> storefront glazing, panelized siding, EIFS and masonry repairs, with access equipment such as lifts and scaffolding.</li>
<li><strong>Interior build-outs:</strong> tenant improvements, ceilings, finishes and fire-protection work after water or smoke.</li>
<li><strong>Code and ordinance items</strong> triggered when a damaged building is brought back into use.</li>
<li><strong>Phasing and access costs</strong> when an occupied building has to stay open during repairs.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Condo and homeowner associations</h2>
<p>Oregon has a large stock of condominiums and planned communities, from Pearl District high-rises to townhome rows in Beaverton. Association claims raise particular questions: what the master policy covers versus unit-owner policies, how the deductible is shared, and how to scope repairs across many units consistently. We work with boards and managers to keep the scope uniform and the documentation clean.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Business income</b>Appraisal clauses in many commercial forms reach business-income losses as an amount-of-loss question. Whether yours does depends on the policy wording, so we confirm it before including income figures in an appraisal.</div>
<h2>Working with your team</h2>
<p>Large losses often involve an engineer, a general contractor, an accountant and sometimes an attorney. We coordinate with each so that the appraisal estimate reflects the same facts everyone else is relying on.</p>

<h2>Questions about commercial &amp; hoa claim appraisal</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Can appraisal resolve a coinsurance penalty dispute?</summary><div class="a"><p>Sometimes. Where the disagreement is about the building’s value or the amount of loss used in the coinsurance calculation, appraisal may help. Disputes about how the clause applies are usually legal questions.</p></div></details><details><summary>Do you work with HOA boards directly?</summary><div class="a"><p>Yes. We usually work with the board and property manager, and we can present findings at a board meeting so owners understand the process.</p></div></details><details><summary>Do you handle churches and nonprofits?</summary><div class="a"><p>Yes. Churches, schools and nonprofit buildings often have specialty finishes and limited budgets for outside help, so we keep fees and scope clear from the start.</p></div></details></div>
